       sscchheedd__ggeett__pprriioorriittyy__mmaaxx() returns the maximum priority value that can be
       used   with   the   scheduling   algorithm   identified   by    _p_o_l_i_c_y.
       sscchheedd__ggeett__pprriioorriittyy__mmiinn() returns the minimum priority value that can be
       used  with  the  scheduling  algorithm identified by _p_o_l_i_c_y.  Supported
       _p_o_l_i_c_y  values  are  SSCCHHEEDD__FFIIFFOO,  SSCCHHEEDD__RRRR,  SSCCHHEEDD__OOTTHHEERR,  SSCCHHEEDD__BBAATTCCHH,
       SSCCHHEEDD__IIDDLLEE,  and  SSCCHHEEDD__DDEEAADDLLIINNEE.  Further details about these policies
       can be found in sscchheedd(7).

       Processes with numerically higher priority values are scheduled  before
       processes  with numerically lower priority values.  Thus, the value re‐
       turned by sscchheedd__ggeett__pprriioorriittyy__mmaaxx() will be greater than the  value  re‐
       turned by sscchheedd__ggeett__pprriioorriittyy__mmiinn().

       Linux  allows  the static priority range 1 to 99 for the SSCCHHEEDD__FFIIFFOO and
       SSCCHHEEDD__RRRR policies, and the  priority  0  for  the  remaining  policies.
       Scheduling priority ranges for the various policies are not alterable.

       The  range  of  scheduling  priorities may vary on other POSIX systems,
       thus it is a good idea for portable applications to use a virtual  pri‐
       ority  range  and  map  it  to  the  interval given by sscchheedd__ggeett__pprriioorr‐‐
